(a) the angles of the triangle are in arithmetic progression;
(b) the common tangent to the nine-point circle and the in-circle is perpendicular to the Euler line.

In a non-equilateral triangle, show that the following are equivalent:
(a) the angles of the triangle are in geometric progression;
(b) the common tangent to the nine-point circle and the in-circle is parallel to the Euler line.

positive_text:
\text{The distance between two circles }\omega\text{ and }\omega'\text{ is defined as the length of their common external tangent and is represented as }d(\omega, \omega').\text{ If two circles don't have a common external tangent, the distance is not defined. A point is a circle with zero radius and the distance can be zero.}

\text{a) Centroid. Let }n\in\mathbb{N}\text{ and circles }\omega_1, \omega_2, ..., \omega_n\text{ be given in the plane. Prove that there exists a unique circle }\bar{\omega}\text{ such that for all circles }\omega\text{ (for which all distances are defined),}
$$\forall \omega : d(\omega, \bar{\omega})^2 = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} d(\omega, \omega_i)^2 + c,\quad\text{with }c\in\mathbb{R} \text{ constant.}$$\text{\bar{\omega} is referred to as the centroid of the given circles.}

negatives.text:
\text{Define }d(\omega, \omega')\text{ as the length of the common internal tangent between circles }\omega\text{ and }\omega';\text{ otherwise undefined. A point is a circle with radius zero.}

\text{a) Centroid. Given circles }\omega_1, \omega_2, ..., \omega_n\text{ in the plane with }n\in\mathbb{N},\text{ prove that a unique circle }\bar{\omega}\text{ exists such that for all circles }\omega\text{ for which all distances are defined,}
$$\forall \omega : d(\omega, \bar{\omega})^2 - \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} d(\omega, \omega_i)^2 = \text{constant.}$$

\text{Define }d(\omega, \omega')\text{ as the length of the common external tangent between circles }\omega\text{ and }\omega';\text{ otherwise undefined. A point is a circle with radius zero.}

\text{a) Centroid. Given circles }\omega_1, \omega_2, ..., \omega_n\text{ in the plane with }n\in\mathbb{N},\text{ prove that a unique circle }\bar{\omega}\text{ exists such that for all circles }\omega\text{ for which all distances are defined,}
$$\forall \omega : d(\omega, \bar{\omega}) - \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} d(\omega, \omega_i) = \text{constant.}$$

